## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses `Object.is` to compare props. Primitives compare by value; objects/functions compare by reference.

```jsx
// Re-renders every time Parent renders — new object reference each time
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />
<Child onClick={() => handleClick()} />
```

Every render creates a *new* object/function literal, so the child always sees "new" props even if the data is identical.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

| Tool | Memoizes | Use when |
|------|----------|----------|
| `React.memo` | Component output | Child receives stable props but re-renders due to parent |
| `useMemo` | Computed value | Expensive calculation or object passed as prop |
| `useCallback` | Function reference | Callback passed to memoized child |

**They only help together.** `React.memo` on a child is useless if the parent passes a new inline function each render.

When *not* to use them: cheap renders, components that always receive new data, or when the memoization overhead exceeds the render cost.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer re-renders when the context *value* changes reference — even if the data is logically the same.

```jsx
// Bad — new object on every Provider render
<MyContext.Provider value={{ user, logout }}>

// Bad — triggers all consumers when only unrelated state changes
const [theme, setTheme] = useState('dark');
const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
<MyContext.Provider value={{ theme, user, setTheme }}>
```

Fix: split contexts by update frequency, or memoize the value.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `List` re-renders on every `App` render, `Item` re-renders on every `List` render:

```jsx
function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>{count}</button>
      <List
        items={['a', 'b', 'c']}          // new array each render
        onSelect={(id) => console.log(id)} // new function each render
      />
    </>
  );
}

function List({ items, onSelect }) {
  return items.map(item => (
    <Item key={item} id={item} onSelect={onSelect} />
  ));
}

function Item({ id, onSelect }) {
  console.log('Item render:', id);
  return <button onClick={() => onSelect(id)}>{id}</button>;
}
```

**After** — `List` and `Item` only re-render when their props actually change:

```jsx
const ITEMS = ['a', 'b', 'c']; // stable reference outside component

function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => {   // stable reference
    console.log(id);
  }, []);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>{count}</button>
      <List items={ITEMS} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const List = React.memo(function List({ items, onSelect }) {
  return items.map(item => (
    <Item key={item} id={item} onSelect={onSelect} />
  ));
});

const Item = React.memo(function Item({ id, onSelect }) {
  console.log('Item render:', id);
  return <button onClick={() => onSelect(id)}>{id}</button>;
});
```

**Key principle:** memoization is a layered contract — stabilize references with `useCallback`/`useMemo`, then prevent propagation with `React.memo`. Missing any layer breaks the whole chain.
